## ***********************************************************************
## **
## **  MENUHELP.INC.BL -- a vertical menu, in BASIC.
## **
## **      MENUHELP.RUN    draw it, drive it, return the row chosen
## **      MENUHELP.DRAW   draw it without driving it
## **      MENUHELP.ROW    one row, in whichever attribute you name
## **
## **  THIS REPLACES THE GP.MENU KEYWORD, which was 462 bytes of assembly
## **  plus 11 of storage in a block every GPB program carries. It was
## **  written in assembly because a highlight had to move without the
## **  runtime knowing what text was underneath it -- so it swapped the
## **  attribute nibbles instead of redrawing. Once the menu itself is
## **  BASIC it OWNS the item array, so it can simply print the row again,
## **  and the reason for the assembly disappears with it.
## **
## **  It is not slower in any way a human can see. The nibble swap was 59
## **  cycles a cell; GP.FILL is 31 and GP.PRINTAT 94, so redrawing two
## **  rows costs about a millisecond against the swap's half. A frame is
## **  16.7ms, and a key repeat is tens of them.
## **
## **  Usage:
## **      MENUHELP.X = 8 : MENUHELP.Y = 6
## **      MENUHELP.WIDTH = 24
## **      MENUHELP.COUNT = 4
## **      MENUHELP.ITEM$(1) = " NEW GAME"    ... and so on
## **      MENUHELP.ATTR = THEME.CLR(THEME.TEXT)
## **      MENUHELP.HIATTR = THEME.CLR(THEME.HILITE)
## **      MENUHELP.HOT$ = "NLOQ"
## **      MENUHELP.HOTATTR = THEME.CLR(THEME.TITLE)
## **      MENUHELP.FLAGS = MENUHELP.GAMEPAD
## **      GOSUB MENUHELP.RUN
## **      IF MENUHELP.SEL = 0 THEN <cancelled>
## **
## **   in   MENUHELP.X        top left of the FIRST ROW, not of a frame --
## **        MENUHELP.Y        draw the box yourself with GP.BOX, so the
## **                          menu owes nothing to one style of border
## **        MENUHELP.WIDTH    cells wide. This is the width of the
## **                          HIGHLIGHT, so it is the width of the menu
## **                          whatever the text happens to do
## **        MENUHELP.COUNT    how many rows
## **        MENUHELP.ITEM$()  the rows, 1..COUNT. THE CALLER OWNS THE DIM
## **                          -- see the note below
## **        MENUHELP.ATTR     packed attribute, background * 16 + fg
## **        MENUHELP.HIATTR   the same for the highlighted row. 0 means
## **                          "invert MENUHELP.ATTR", which is what
## **                          GP.MENU always did
## **        MENUHELP.HOT$     one character a row, "" for none. Matched
## **                          WITHOUT case, and a hotkey CHOOSES its row
## **                          rather than moving to it -- which is what
## **                          makes hotkeys worth having
## **        MENUHELP.HOTATTR  paint the hotkey LETTER in this attribute, so
## **                          the shortcut is visible in the row instead of
## **                          being a secret. 0 is off, and off is the
## **                          default, so no existing menu changes.
## **                          The letter is found in the row's own text --
## **                          the first match for that row's HOT$
## **                          character, without case -- so nothing extra
## **                          is passed in and " START" with hotkey S
## **                          needs no markup. A row whose text does not
## **                          contain its hotkey is simply left alone.
## **                          ONLY ROWS IN THE NORMAL ATTRIBUTE ARE
## **                          TINTED: on the selected row the highlight IS
## **                          the message, and a second colour inside the
## **                          bar is noise. It also means this never has to
## **                          guess how to invert a caller's HIATTR.
## **        MENUHELP.FLAGS    added together, see below
## **        MENUHELP.SEL      the row to start on; 0 starts at 1
## **
## **   out  MENUHELP.SEL      1..COUNT, or 0 if cancelled
## **        MENUHELP.KEY      the key code that ended it: 13 chose, 27
## **                          cancelled, or the hotkey itself
## **
## **  THE CALLER OWNS THE DIM, deliberately. A module cannot be handed an
## **  array in BASIC, so it has to name one -- and if it DIMmed that array
## **  itself it would have to guess a bound, then fail on the caller who
## **  wanted more. Leave MENUHELP.ITEM$ undimensioned and GPC's implicit
## **  DIM gives you 0..10, which is a ten row menu for free; DIM it and
## **  you can have as many as you like. What you must NOT do is both.
## **
## **  X and Y keep their one letter names because a coordinate pair is
## **  the one place where that IS the descriptive name. Everything else
## **  is spelled out.
## **
## **  The whole MENUHELP.* name space belongs to this file.
## **
## ***********************************************************************
#IFNDEF MENUHELP.DEFS
#DEFINE MENUHELP.DEFS 1

## Flags. MUSTSEL takes ESC away, for a menu with nothing behind it to go
## back to. KEEPMARK leaves the chosen row highlighted, for when the menu
## stays on screen while whatever was chosen happens. NOWRAP stops at the
## ends instead of rolling round, which suits a list that reads as a range.
## GAMEPAD adds the SNES pad in port 1 to the keyboard; it does not replace
## it, so a program never has to ask which the user has.

#DEFINE MENUHELP.MUSTSEL 1
#DEFINE MENUHELP.KEEPMARK 2
#DEFINE MENUHELP.NOWRAP 4
#DEFINE MENUHELP.GAMEPAD 8

## PETSCII, as GET reports it.

#DEFINE MENUHELP.DOWN 17
#DEFINE MENUHELP.UP 145
#DEFINE MENUHELP.ENTER 13
#DEFINE MENUHELP.ESCAPE 27
#DEFINE MENUHELP.STOP 3
#DEFINE MENUHELP.SPACE 32

## SNES port 1. Port 0 is the keyboard pretending to be a joystick, which a
## menu does not want -- it reads the cursor keys twice.

#DEFINE MENUHELP.PORT 1

## The bits of JOY's low byte, which is already active high. Left and right
## mean nothing to a vertical menu and are ignored rather than mapped to
## something invented.
##   $80 B   $10 START   $08 UP   $04 DOWN   $02 LEFT   $01 RIGHT

#DEFINE MENUHELP.PAD.UP 8
#DEFINE MENUHELP.PAD.DOWN 4
#DEFINE MENUHELP.PAD.B 128
#DEFINE MENUHELP.PAD.START 16

GOTO MENUHELP.MODULE.END

## ***********************************************************************
## **  MENUHELP.RUN -- the whole thing.
## ***********************************************************************

MENUHELP.RUN:
    MENUHELP.KEY = 0
    IF MENUHELP.COUNT < 1 THEN MENUHELP.SEL = 0 : RETURN

## ---- 0 for the highlight means "invert the normal attribute", which is
## ---- exactly what GP.MENU did to every cell it touched. A caller that
## ---- wants a colour of its own just says so.

    MENUHELP.HIGHLIGHT = MENUHELP.HIATTR
    IF MENUHELP.HIATTR = 0 THEN MENUHELP.HIGHLIGHT = (MENUHELP.ATTR AND 15) * 16 + INT(MENUHELP.ATTR / 16)

    IF MENUHELP.SEL < 1 THEN MENUHELP.SEL = 1
    IF MENUHELP.SEL > MENUHELP.COUNT THEN MENUHELP.SEL = MENUHELP.COUNT

    MENUHELP.PADHELD = 0 : GOSUB MENUHELP.DRAW
    MENUHELP.DRAWROW = MENUHELP.SEL : MENUHELP.DRAWATTR = MENUHELP.HIGHLIGHT
    GOSUB MENUHELP.ROW

## ---- Wait. GET comes straight back with "" when nothing is pressed, so
## ---- the pad is polled in the same loop rather than on a timer -- and
## ---- the keyboard is read FIRST, so a plugged in pad never costs a
## ---- keystroke its response.

MENUHELP.WAIT:
    GET MENUHELP.INCHAR$
    IF MENUHELP.INCHAR$ <> "" THEN MENUHELP.CODE = ASC(MENUHELP.INCHAR$) : GOTO MENUHELP.KEYED
    IF (MENUHELP.FLAGS AND MENUHELP.GAMEPAD) = 0 THEN GOTO MENUHELP.WAIT
    GOSUB MENUHELP.PADKEY
    IF MENUHELP.CODE = 0 THEN GOTO MENUHELP.WAIT

## ---- One key. The sparse selector GP.SELECT exists for: five control
## ---- codes scattered across 256, and everything else falling through to
## ---- the hotkeys.
## ----
## ---- NOTHING BELOW DOES GOTO OUT OF THE SELECT. GP.ENDSEL is what
## ---- releases the selector's frame, so jumping past it would leak one on
## ---- every keystroke. MENUHELP.DONE is tested after GP.ENDSEL instead:
## ---- one comparison, and it cannot leak.

MENUHELP.KEYED:
    MENUHELP.DONE = 0
    MENUHELP.PREVSEL = MENUHELP.SEL
    GP.SELECT MENUHELP.CODE
        GP.CASE MENUHELP.UP
            MENUHELP.SEL = MENUHELP.SEL - 1
            IF MENUHELP.SEL < 1 THEN GOSUB MENUHELP.WRAPTOP
        GP.CASE MENUHELP.DOWN
            MENUHELP.SEL = MENUHELP.SEL + 1
            IF MENUHELP.SEL > MENUHELP.COUNT THEN GOSUB MENUHELP.WRAPBOT
        GP.CASE MENUHELP.ENTER
            MENUHELP.DONE = 1
        GP.CASE MENUHELP.ESCAPE, MENUHELP.STOP
            GOSUB MENUHELP.CANCEL
        GP.ELSE
            GOSUB MENUHELP.HOTKEY
    GP.ENDSEL

## ---- Move the highlight only if it actually moved. Two rows redrawn, and
## ---- the old one goes back to exactly what MENUHELP.DRAW put there,
## ---- because both come from the same MENUHELP.ROW.

    IF MENUHELP.SEL = MENUHELP.PREVSEL THEN GOTO MENUHELP.SETTLE
    IF MENUHELP.PREVSEL < 1 THEN GOTO MENUHELP.SETTLE
    MENUHELP.DRAWROW = MENUHELP.PREVSEL : MENUHELP.DRAWATTR = MENUHELP.ATTR
    GOSUB MENUHELP.ROW
    IF MENUHELP.SEL < 1 THEN GOTO MENUHELP.SETTLE
    MENUHELP.DRAWROW = MENUHELP.SEL : MENUHELP.DRAWATTR = MENUHELP.HIGHLIGHT
    GOSUB MENUHELP.ROW

MENUHELP.SETTLE:
    IF MENUHELP.DONE = 0 THEN GOTO MENUHELP.WAIT
    MENUHELP.KEY = MENUHELP.CODE

## ---- Leave the screen the way the caller expects to find it. Unless
## ---- KEEPMARK is set the highlight comes off, so a menu that has done
## ---- its job is not still claiming the user's attention.

    IF (MENUHELP.FLAGS AND MENUHELP.KEEPMARK) <> 0 THEN RETURN
    IF MENUHELP.SEL < 1 THEN RETURN
    MENUHELP.DRAWROW = MENUHELP.SEL : MENUHELP.DRAWATTR = MENUHELP.ATTR
    GOSUB MENUHELP.ROW
    RETURN

## ***********************************************************************
## **  The three things the selector's cases needed a subroutine for.
## **
## **  Wrapping is a GOSUB rather than a line inside the case because the
## **  no-wrap answer is not "stay put", it is "go to the end you are at" --
## **  and after MENUHELP.SEL has already been stepped past it, that is two
## **  statements either way.
## ***********************************************************************

MENUHELP.WRAPTOP:
    MENUHELP.SEL = MENUHELP.COUNT
    IF (MENUHELP.FLAGS AND MENUHELP.NOWRAP) <> 0 THEN MENUHELP.SEL = 1
    RETURN

MENUHELP.WRAPBOT:
    MENUHELP.SEL = 1
    IF (MENUHELP.FLAGS AND MENUHELP.NOWRAP) <> 0 THEN MENUHELP.SEL = MENUHELP.COUNT
    RETURN

## ---- MUSTSEL swallows ESC entirely. Note it does not beep or flash: a
## ---- menu that cannot be left says so by not leaving.

MENUHELP.CANCEL:
    IF (MENUHELP.FLAGS AND MENUHELP.MUSTSEL) <> 0 THEN RETURN
    MENUHELP.SEL = 0 : MENUHELP.DONE = 1 : RETURN

## ***********************************************************************
## **  MENUHELP.HOTKEY -- anything that was not a cursor key.
## **
## **  Matched without case, by folding both sides to upper -- the same
## **  a-z-only fold GP.COMP and GP.SORT use, so digits, punctuation and
## **  PETSCII graphics all pass through and can all be hotkeys.
## **
## **  The scan stops at MENUHELP.COUNT even if MENUHELP.HOT$ is longer, so a
## **  hotkey string left over from a longer menu cannot select a row that
## **  is not on the screen.
## ***********************************************************************

MENUHELP.HOTKEY:
    IF MENUHELP.HOT$ = "" THEN RETURN
    MENUHELP.HOTLAST = LEN(MENUHELP.HOT$)
    IF MENUHELP.HOTLAST > MENUHELP.COUNT THEN MENUHELP.HOTLAST = MENUHELP.COUNT
    MENUHELP.WANTCODE = MENUHELP.CODE
    IF MENUHELP.WANTCODE > 96 THEN GOSUB MENUHELP.FOLDWANT
    FOR MENUHELP.SCAN = 1 TO MENUHELP.HOTLAST
        MENUHELP.HOTCODE = ASC(MID$(MENUHELP.HOT$, MENUHELP.SCAN, 1))
        IF MENUHELP.HOTCODE > 96 THEN GOSUB MENUHELP.FOLDHC
        IF MENUHELP.HOTCODE = MENUHELP.WANTCODE THEN MENUHELP.SEL = MENUHELP.SCAN : MENUHELP.DONE = 1
    NEXT
    RETURN

## ---- Written as a guarded subroutine rather than one ANDed condition on
## ---- purpose. AND sits BELOW the comparisons in the precedence table, so
## ---- a single line would be right by luck rather than by design -- and
## ---- that is the kind of thing that shows up on the one code it gets
## ---- wrong. The caller has already established the value is above 96.

MENUHELP.FOLDWANT:
    IF MENUHELP.WANTCODE < 123 THEN MENUHELP.WANTCODE = MENUHELP.WANTCODE - 32
    RETURN

MENUHELP.FOLDHC:
    IF MENUHELP.HOTCODE < 123 THEN MENUHELP.HOTCODE = MENUHELP.HOTCODE - 32
    RETURN

## ***********************************************************************
## **  MENUHELP.PADKEY -- the SNES pad as a key code.
## **
## **  Only NEW presses act. Without the edge test a held direction would
## **  run the menu round and round at the speed of the BASIC loop, which
## **  is far too fast to stop on the row you wanted.
## **
## **  B and START both choose, whichever a player reaches for.
## **
## **  out  MENUHELP.CODE   a PETSCII code, or 0 for nothing new
## ***********************************************************************

MENUHELP.PADKEY:
    MENUHELP.CODE = 0
    GOSUB MENUHELP.PADREAD
    MENUHELP.PADNEW = MENUHELP.PADNOW AND (255 - MENUHELP.PADHELD)
    MENUHELP.PADHELD = MENUHELP.PADNOW
    IF (MENUHELP.PADNEW AND MENUHELP.PAD.UP) <> 0 THEN MENUHELP.CODE = MENUHELP.UP : RETURN
    IF (MENUHELP.PADNEW AND MENUHELP.PAD.DOWN) <> 0 THEN MENUHELP.CODE = MENUHELP.DOWN : RETURN
    IF (MENUHELP.PADNEW AND MENUHELP.PAD.B) <> 0 THEN MENUHELP.CODE = MENUHELP.ENTER : RETURN
    IF (MENUHELP.PADNEW AND MENUHELP.PAD.START) <> 0 THEN MENUHELP.CODE = MENUHELP.ENTER
    RETURN

## ---- JOY IS A GPC BUILT IN and does all of this already: it calls the
## ---- KERNAL's joystick_get, tests for no hardware, and inverts both
## ---- bytes, so what comes back is active HIGH and the bits above read the
## ---- right way round. An earlier version of this routine did the same
## ---- thing by hand with GP.CALL to $FF56 -- pointless, and it made the
## ---- module depend on the machine code interface for nothing.
## ----
## ---- NEGATIVE MEANS NO PAD IS THERE. It has to be tested: with no pad the
## ---- raw bytes are $FF and inverted that is 0, so the value would look
## ---- like a pad with nothing pressed, which is right only by accident.
## ----
## ---- The low byte carries the directions, B and START. Extracted by
## ---- division, NOT by "AND 255" -- AND is 16 bit SIGNED in GPC and both
## ---- pad bytes together run past 32767.

MENUHELP.PADREAD:
    MENUHELP.PADNOW = 0 : MENUHELP.PADRAW = JOY(MENUHELP.PORT)
    IF MENUHELP.PADRAW < 0 THEN RETURN
    MENUHELP.PADNOW = MENUHELP.PADRAW - INT(MENUHELP.PADRAW / 256) * 256
    RETURN

## ***********************************************************************
## **  MENUHELP.DRAW -- every row, unhighlighted.
## **
## **  Public, because a caller that put a dialog over the menu and did not
## **  stash what was underneath needs it back. It draws ROWS ONLY, never a
## **  frame, so calling it again cannot damage a border.
## ***********************************************************************

MENUHELP.DRAW:
    FOR MENUHELP.EACH = 1 TO MENUHELP.COUNT
        MENUHELP.DRAWROW = MENUHELP.EACH : MENUHELP.DRAWATTR = MENUHELP.ATTR
        GOSUB MENUHELP.ROW
    NEXT
    RETURN

## ***********************************************************************
## **  MENUHELP.ROW -- one row, filled then printed.
## **
## **   in   MENUHELP.DRAWROW    the row, 1..N        MENUHELP.DRAWATTR   the attribute
## **
## **  THE FILL IS WHAT MAKES THE HIGHLIGHT THE FULL WIDTH, and it is also
## **  why no padded string is built here: GP.FILL colours all W cells for
## **  31 cycles each, where LEFT$(item$ + "        ", W) would allocate a
## **  string on every keystroke to achieve the same thing.
## **
## **  Text longer than W is cut, and only then -- the trim costs an
## **  allocation, so it is not paid by the normal case. Without it the
## **  overflow would not stop at the edge of the menu; VERA simply carries
## **  on into the next cells, which is the next line of the screen.
## ***********************************************************************

MENUHELP.ROW:
    MENUHELP.DRAWY = MENUHELP.Y + MENUHELP.DRAWROW - 1
    GP.FILL MENUHELP.X, MENUHELP.DRAWY, MENUHELP.WIDTH, 1, MENUHELP.SPACE, MENUHELP.DRAWATTR
    MENUHELP.DRAWTEXT$ = MENUHELP.ITEM$(MENUHELP.DRAWROW)
    IF MENUHELP.DRAWTEXT$ = "" THEN RETURN
    IF LEN(MENUHELP.DRAWTEXT$) > MENUHELP.WIDTH THEN MENUHELP.DRAWTEXT$ = LEFT$(MENUHELP.DRAWTEXT$, MENUHELP.WIDTH)
    GP.PRINTAT MENUHELP.X, MENUHELP.DRAWY, MENUHELP.DRAWTEXT$, MENUHELP.DRAWATTR

## ---- The hotkey letter, in its own attribute, over the top of the row
## ---- just printed. One cell, and only when the caller asked for it -- a
## ---- menu that sets no HOTATTR pays a single compare per row.
## ----
## ---- The highlighted row is skipped: it is drawn in an attribute that is
## ---- not MENUHELP.ATTR, which is exactly the test below. See the note in
## ---- the interface block for why that is a decision and not an omission.

    IF MENUHELP.HOTATTR = 0 THEN RETURN
    IF MENUHELP.DRAWATTR <> MENUHELP.ATTR THEN RETURN
    GOSUB MENUHELP.HOTFIND
    IF MENUHELP.HOTAT = 0 THEN RETURN
    GP.PRINTAT MENUHELP.X + MENUHELP.HOTAT - 1, MENUHELP.DRAWY, MID$(MENUHELP.DRAWTEXT$, MENUHELP.HOTAT, 1), MENUHELP.HOTATTR
    RETURN

## ***********************************************************************
## **  MENUHELP.HOTFIND -- where this row's hotkey letter sits in its text.
## **
## **   in   MENUHELP.DRAWROW  MENUHELP.DRAWTEXT$
## **   out  MENUHELP.HOTAT    1 based position, or 0 for "do not tint"
## **
## **  Zero is returned for every ordinary reason a tint should not happen:
## **  no hotkey string, no hotkey for THIS row, or a row whose text simply
## **  does not contain its own hotkey. That last one is a caller's choice,
## **  not an error -- " QUIT" with hotkey X is still a working hotkey.
## **
## **  ITS OWN SCAN VARIABLES, not the ones MENUHELP.HOTKEY uses. The two
## **  never run at once today -- a keypress is fully resolved before any
## **  row is redrawn -- but sharing a loop counter across two routines is
## **  a trap set for whoever changes that order later.
## ***********************************************************************

MENUHELP.HOTFIND:
    MENUHELP.HOTAT = 0
    IF MENUHELP.HOT$ = "" THEN RETURN
    IF MENUHELP.DRAWROW > LEN(MENUHELP.HOT$) THEN RETURN
    MENUHELP.HOTWANT = ASC(MID$(MENUHELP.HOT$, MENUHELP.DRAWROW, 1))
    IF MENUHELP.HOTWANT > 96 THEN GOSUB MENUHELP.FOLDHOTWANT
    FOR MENUHELP.HOTSCAN = 1 TO LEN(MENUHELP.DRAWTEXT$)
        MENUHELP.HOTHERE = ASC(MID$(MENUHELP.DRAWTEXT$, MENUHELP.HOTSCAN, 1))
        IF MENUHELP.HOTHERE > 96 THEN GOSUB MENUHELP.FOLDHOTHERE
        IF MENUHELP.HOTHERE = MENUHELP.HOTWANT AND MENUHELP.HOTAT = 0 THEN MENUHELP.HOTAT = MENUHELP.HOTSCAN
    NEXT
    RETURN

## ---- Guarded subroutines and not one ANDed line, for the reason given at
## ---- MENUHELP.FOLDWANT: AND sits below the comparisons in the precedence
## ---- table, so the single line version would be right by luck.

MENUHELP.FOLDHOTWANT:
    IF MENUHELP.HOTWANT < 123 THEN MENUHELP.HOTWANT = MENUHELP.HOTWANT - 32
    RETURN

MENUHELP.FOLDHOTHERE:
    IF MENUHELP.HOTHERE < 123 THEN MENUHELP.HOTHERE = MENUHELP.HOTHERE - 32
    RETURN

MENUHELP.MODULE.END:
#ENDIF
